Making a plugin command in MZ isn't that difficult. The actual functional code would look something like this:
JavaScript:
const me = 'YourPluginName';
PluginManager.registerCommand(me, 'CommandName', function(args) {
	$gameVariables.setSelfValue([args.mapId, args.eventId, args.selfSwitch], args.value);
});
Then you'd need all the comment syntax to tell the editor how to handle your new command. That syntax would need to define what mapID, eventId, selfSwitch, and value actually mean in the above code.

When you run a script call directly inside an event, the game knows
which event
is running it. So this.eventId() works, because “this” is the event’s interpreter — basically the little engine that’s executing that event’s commands.
But when you put the same code inside a plugin command, the meaning of “this” changes. In your version, you used an arrow function. Arrow functions don’t create their own “this” — they just grab whatever “this” was outside. In this case, that’s the plugin file itself, not the event interpreter. So when you call this.eventId(), the game goes: “Wait, I don’t know what event you’re talking about,” and it crashes.
The fix is to use a normal function instead of an arrow function. Normal functions let RPG Maker bind “this” to the interpreter that’s actually running the event. That way, when you call this.eventId(), it correctly gives you the ID of the event that triggered the plugin command.
/*:
* @target MZ
* @plugindesc Adds commands to spawn and erase events.
*
@Author
Chris Cote
*
* @command EventEraseSelf
*
@desc
Erase the event running the command, and remove saved data for that event.
*/
(() => {
PluginManager.registerCommand("SpawnEventCommands", "EventEraseSelf", function(args) {
// Here, "this" is the Game_Interpreter running the event
const eventId = this.eventId();
Ritter.unspawnEvent(eventId, true);
});
})();
Use function expression (function(args) { ... }) instead of an arrow function.
Arrow functions don’t bind their own this, so they inherit from the outer scope (the plugin file), which is why you were getting the wrong context.A normal function gets its this bound to the interpreter when called by the engine.Inside that function, this.eventId() will correctly return the ID of the event that ran the plugin command.You can then pass that ID into your Ritter.unspawnEvent call.
If you ever need the map ID  as well, you can grab it from this._mapId inside the interpreter. For example:
const mapId = this._mapId;
That way you can uniquely identify the event across maps if your system needs it.
Hope this fix your issue and i explained in a way you understand.

